WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.735
Test data can create real risk when production data is copied into lower environments.

2
00:00:05.735 --> 00:00:18.856
Development, testing, demos, training, and troubleshooting environments often have broader access, weaker monitoring, relaxed change control, and more experimental tooling than production.

3
00:00:18.856 --> 00:00:21.606
Real data should not move there casually.

4
00:00:21.606 --> 00:00:25.770
Prefer synthetic, masked, tokenized, or minimized datasets.

5
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:36.691
Test logs, screenshots, fixtures, snapshots, exported databases, and CI artifacts should not contain real sensitive data unless that use is explicitly governed.

6
00:00:36.691 --> 00:00:43.684
A useful test dataset should support engineering work without creating a second production data estate.

7
00:00:43.684 --> 00:00:45.962
Retention should be intentional.

8
00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:52.484
Keep sensitive data only as long as needed for business, legal, operational, or security reasons.

9
00:00:52.484 --> 00:01:02.069
Deletion workflows should consider databases, files, caches, queues, logs, backups, analytics stores, search indexes, and derived records.

10
00:01:02.069 --> 00:01:07.569
A row deleted from the main database may still exist in several operational copies.

11
00:01:07.569 --> 00:01:17.076
Privacy and security review should confirm data categories, purposes, flows, access rules, retention, redaction, and incident procedures.

12
00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:26.111
That review should happen before release and again when features add new data, new integrations, new exports, or new support workflows.

13
00:01:26.111 --> 00:01:38.760
Incident readiness should include evidence preservation, containment, notification paths, owner identification, credential rotation, data impact review, and follow-up remediation.

14
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:46.303
Sensitive data protection is stronger when the team already knows who makes decisions and where the data might be.
